ons outside  organizations with connections to Russia  working hard to influence the Trump  campaign or provide avenues of  communications promising different to  deliver on different aspects of sort of  policy changes all of this is absolutely  horrific  and it has been this annoying drip drip  drip I hate that phrase but plus it is  over the last few years but the body of  this is indicative of a systemic problem  relating to this campaign and the people  involved in it it could just be because  many of them have their own ethical and  corruption related issues that will  probably come out over time we've seen  some of that in the beginning of this  cabinet the number of people that have  been challenged on ethical issues many  of them are extremely wealthy and have  connections to Russian businesses before  whatever but I think it's more than that  and I think you see this sort of  building up that there was a systemic  and sort of very systematic effort from  the Russians to find avenues of  influence in the United States to  achieve very specific political  objectives and strategic goals  and it just so happened that there were  many open doors when they started  pushing on them around the Trump  Organization and the Trump campaign so I  think more of that will come out and  it's and none of it is going to be very  pretty but I think that the sort of  collective body of all of that is  already very very damning so one of the  talking points so if there's sort of  like a spectrum of opinion as on this  entire thing there is people who say  there's there's literally just nothing  here at all the witch-hunt sort of crowd  on the other side you have people who  are waiting for Trump to be forcefully  removed from office in some way as a  result of what comes from from this  investigation there's a specific sliver  in there though that says that okay that  there has been wrongdoing turned up by  this investigation but none of it  relates directly to Donald Trump and to  Donald Trump's campaign that it's stuff  that people around him did before or  it's things that haven't been  specifically tied to Trump himself what  is the truth of that as a general claim  so I think as a sort of very general  claim you can say well none of this has  Trump himself yet but I think that is  not I mean even from the details in the  current indictments the implication is  that that's not true that he's connected  to many of these activities certainly on  the business side of looking at the  attempts to make business deals with  Russia he was engaged in those  activities he knew about them he knew he  knew what was going on he was making  statements in direct connection to the  efforts to achieve a business deal with  Russia during his campaign you know I  think on his side there seems to be  already and from some of his lawyers who  are not cartoons there seems to be this  effort to lay the groundwork for to be  able to say he didn't know about any of  this this was all volunteerism on the  part of the people around him including  his own children and you know this guy  was just running for office and and what  he said himself which was well I thought  I was going to lose so you know why  shouldn't I try to make money while I'm  campaigning for president knowing that  I'm not going to win now even that is an  incredibly ridiculous statement in terms  of the ethics of public office and  seeking public office and the  corruption that he is himself implying  he is engaged in but that will be I  think a core lane of effort that they  will try to use to convince people that  he has nothing to do with this  I don't ultimately think that will be  successful because what we have seen in  every one of the indictments from Muller  and his team so far is there is an  incredible amount of data backing what  they do it's not he said she said it's  not some guy over here gave us an  interview  it's intercept material it is  communications emails phone calls t