an be proven  related to the campaign I think the the  focus for the intention or for the  purposes of the Moller investigation  should very much be that latter piece it  should be what was happening between you  know 2014 and 2017 it looks like they've  been investigating some things after he  was sworn into office as well so sort of  anything since he became focused on  running for president in the mid you  know 2010 I think should be fair game  before that I mean a lot of it is  context that's helpful I think the but  the piece of this that I think is often  ignored is yes there you know there were  contacts between Trump and the Soviet  Union various Soviet individuals in New  York when he went to the Soviet Union to  visit on an ongoing via his first wife  Ivana whose father was sort of reporting  on him as an up-and-coming businessman  to local intelligence operatives back in  the Czech Republic or Czechoslovakia at  the time you know there's there's a lot  of different lines of this but I think  if you look at the early 90s there was  sort of a core ideological concept for  the Kremlin of okay how do we rebuild  all of this influence that we've lost in  the world since the collapse of the  Soviet Union it wasn't just the loss of  territory it was the loss of sort of  greatness of being a big force at the  table of being able to have access to  everything in different groups and they  really made an effort to gain access to  cultural elites especially business and  financial elites around the world to  find these places where the resources  they had particularly in the business  and financial world could gain them  access back to decision makers and  policy makers and that's always been a  long line of effort they don't make  friends with a guy in New York and say  okay we're gonna make you president in  30 years they make friends with people  in New York knowing that most of those  people will just sort of continue on but  maybe three or four of those and  up in positions that are potentially  useful over time I think Trump was very  much on that list he was bombastic if  you know the sort of recruitment and  influence acronym is mice money ideology  compromise and ego I think in in every  aspect of that there's a way to  influence Trump in different ways I  think they knew that over time but I  think that it was initially I do believe  it was the sort of this is somebody they  could potentially be useful and that  probably evolved into something else  later on but what that tipping point was  I'm not really sure yeah and and we may  not exactly find out what that tipping  point was we've been speaking with Molly  McHugh foreign policy and strategy  consultant also an expert on information  warfare Molly great is always to talk to  you thanks for having me

